# Native state and buffer reuse in a CPU-backed KV cache

Anonymous working manuscript — evidence freeze, 3 October 2026.
This manuscript reports supported component outcomes and a negative baseline comparison. It is not a completed submission: independent headline reproduction, independent quality, generality and physical-device validation remain open.

## Abstract

We evaluate a BF16 KV-cache prototype with a CPU DRAM cold tier, native transfers, fixed native CPU state and pooled GPU metadata. Native state phases preserve the existing retention policy on tested trajectories. Allocating final shared visible/hot buffers during per-layer prefill reduces fresh-process prefill allocation by11.22% relative to the same CPU policy's global placement. A fixed16K prompt/50-step faithful SnapKV comparison nevertheless finds54.59 versus25.25ms/token, with worse p95. Component memory improvements therefore do not establish a joint quality/latency/resource win. We retain failed outcomes and distinguish CPU DRAM measurements from unmeasured physical STT behavior.

## Implementation and measurement

The prototype stores grouped-head BF16 cold KV in owned pinned CPU roots and uses native batched transfers. Canonical native CPU arrays own hot/cold positions, grace, shadow/resident flags, streaks and logical/free physical slot order. Fixed GPU owners hold score/age/timestamp planes. Early shared prefill places each layer into its final visible/hot root while current attention consumes the original full prompt KV; the global handoff fills future cold tails. Unsupported multi-token appends use the ordinary scoring/state fallback and return to the native single-token path on tested cases.

Tests use a local Mistral-7B-Instruct model, BF16, one seed and an L40S GPU. Resource audits use fresh processes, constant-model storage subtraction, phase allocation peaks, owned pinned allocator blocks and explicit native CPU array roots. This measured scope excludes unowned host memory and driver allocations. Predictions, state, counters and native KV payload/calls are compared to frozen same-configuration controls. Tiny GPU tests additionally compare full logits and payload bits and inject a partial-prefill exception to verify cleanup. Full-model teacher replay verifies state and predicted tokens; it does not independently measure task quality.

The paired latency study uses one fixed16K prompt,50 teacher-forced decode forwards, one warm-up per arm and three ABBA blocks. Each arm has300 measured forwards. Host-visible token synchronization and required policy work are charged. Twenty-five before/after GPU process observations check interference; these are not continuous utilization monitoring. Diagnostic resource observations and state hashes are outside measured step timing.

