## Native cold-boundary follow-up

An opt-in native cold-boundary implementation directly gathers F32 timestamp rows, preserves the original grouped ranking and selected physical row order, uses the same mapped nativeH2D payload transfer, and scatters cold tails in one GPU launch. Three stationary3ABBA cases at hot649/991, including ragged tied/NaN timestamps, save4.87–6.49ms with exact tested payload/selection/metadata/native transfer parity. Nondefault-stream lifetime and atomic preflight rejection pass. These are stage timings, not achieved model gains. [Stage proof](results/hardware/native_cold_boundary_v1/verified_stage.json).

The518-test suite and integrated tiny full-logit/cache tests pass, including multi-token fallback. Full-model4K/12 and16K/50 preserve predictions/state/counters/native bytes/calls; all50 ordinary steps use native cold composition. Scoped memory is424.15MiB, charging conservative descriptor/index scratch bounds; strict GPU active allocation, reservation and global-prefill peak remain unchanged versus the bulk control. Failure after coldH2D submission cleans up owned pinned storage, events, hooks and leases. [Resource proof](results/hardware/cold_boundary_controller_v1/verified_resources.json).

The preregistered same-bulk-control whole-model speed experiment has no measured outcome: its quiet guard found an unrelated GPU0 process before model load and aborted with zero rows. The attempt is preserved; other users' processes were untouched. Mandatory candidate stress awaits a valid primary speed result. No quality, faithful-baseline or production adoption claim follows from these tests.

